However, adding Z2 ~~ Z1 manually will lead to this problem:

path_sd2_manual <-

'Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
 Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2

 Z1 ~~ Z.int + Z2

 x1.indirect. := a1 * b1

 x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - 5.403299 * l)
 x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + 5.403299 * l)
 '

fit_sd2_manual <- sem(path_sd2_manual, dat, fixed.x = FALSE)
lavInspect(fit_sd2_manual, "est")$psi
#       Z1      Y       x1      x2      Z.int   Z2
# Z1     10.546
# Y       0.000   3.261
# x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
# x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
# Z.int  -5.731   0.000   0.000   0.000 452.022
# Z2      1.532   0.000   0.000   0.000   0.000  28.904

All the other covariances are fixed to zero. We need to free them manually.

I wrote a simple function, add_exo_cov() in the package semhelpinghands, for this case:

https://sfcheung.github.io/semhelpinghands/reference/auto_cov.html

library(semhelpinghands)
path_sd2_manual2 <- add_exo_cov(path_sd2_manual)
fit_sd2_manual2 <- sem(path_sd2_manual2, dat, fixed.x = FALSE)
# Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
#  Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2
#  Z1 ~~ Z.int + Z2
#  x1.indirect. := a1 * b1
#  x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - 5.403299 * l)
#  x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + 5.403299 * l)
#
# # Added by auto_exo_cov
# x1 ~~ x2 + Z.int + Z2
# x2 ~~ Z.int + Z2
# Z.int ~~ Z2
fit_sd2_manual2 <- sem(path_sd2_manual2, dat, fixed.x = FALSE)
lavInspect(fit_sd2_manual2, "est")$psi
#       Z1      Y       x1      x2      Z.int   Z2
# Z1     10.540
# Y       0.000   3.261
# x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
# x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
# Z.int  -5.664   0.000  -2.125  -0.070 452.021
# Z2      1.436   0.000  -0.685   3.881   1.037  28.904

Note that the function is just a helper. Users should check the generated syntax and modify it if necessary.

If there is a built-in solution in lavaan for cases like this, I would love to know.

-- Shu Fai

