> > > 2022년 10월 6일 목요일 오후 12시 28분 59초 UTC-5에 Wen L.님이 작성:
> > > > Hi,
> > > >
> > > > It depends on how your moderator Z2 is coded; specifically how to interpret a zero value. E.g., If Z2 is mean-centered, then the indirect effect of X1 on Y via Z1, at the mean value of Z2, would be a1*b1. The indirect effect of X1 on Y via Z1, at a value of Z2 one standard deviation above the mean, would be a1*(b1+I). These follow from noting that the predictors I*(Z1*Z2) + b1*Z1 = (I*Z2 + b1)*Z1 in the outcome model.
> > > >
> > > > You should also include the treatments x1 and x2 in your outcome model, e.g., 'Y ~ I*Z.int+b1*Z1+b2*Z2+c1*x1+c2*x2' even if you're not interested in the direct effects because they can be common causes of Z1 and Y. And I would suggest including confounders of Z1 and Y as well (assuming X1 and X2 are randomized) .
