Dear Heysung,

This issue is actually much more complicated that it appears to be, if our concern is not just on point estimates but on the standard errors, p-values, and confidence intervals. The issue about standardizedSolution() vs standardization-first, a more general issue, is being discussed in another thread and so I will focus on conditional indirect effects here.

I repeat below the methods presented before, with both the version using the variance of Z2 in the model and the version shared by Wen Wei that standardizes Z2 first:

options(width = 132)

set.seed(87452)
n <- 100
x1 <- rnorm(n)
x2 <- rnorm(n)
Z1 <- .3 * x1 + .4 * x2 + rnorm(n, 0, .8)
Z2 <- rnorm(n)
Y <- .1 * Z1 + .2 * Z2 + .3 * Z1 * Z2 + rnorm(n, 0, .9)
# Arbitrarily scale them
dat <- data.frame(x1 = x1 * 2,
                  x2 = x2 * 3,
                  Z1 = Z1 * 4,
                  Z2 = Z2 * 5,
                  Y = Y * 2)
# Mean center all variables
dat <- as.data.frame(scale(dat, center = TRUE, scale = FALSE))
# Form product term
dat$Z.int <- (dat$Z1 * dat$Z2)

library(lavaan)
#> This is lavaan 0.6-12
#> lavaan is FREE software! Please report any bugs.

# Mean-Centering Only
Z2_sd <- sd(dat$Z2)
# Need to hard code the SD of Z2 because Z2 will not be
# treated as fixed if we label the variance of Z2
# as in the previous post.
path_center_only <-
paste0(

'Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
 Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2
 x1.indirect. := a1 * b1

 x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - ', Z2_sd,' * l)
 x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + ', Z2_sd,' * l)
 ')
cat(path_center_only)
#> Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
#>  Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2
#>  x1.indirect. := a1 * b1
#>  x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - 5.40329947268516 * l)
#>  x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + 5.40329947268516 * l)
#>
fit_center_only <- sem(path_center_only, dat)

# Standardize Z2 First
dat$Z2_std <- scale(dat$Z2)
dat$Z.std.int <http://Z.std.int> <- (dat$Z1 * dat$Z2_std)

path_std <-

'Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2

 Y  ~ l * Z.std.int <http://Z.std.int> + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2_std + x1 + x2

 x1.indirect. := a1 * b1

 x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - l)
 x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + l)
 '
fit_std <- sem(path_std, dat)

print(parameterEstimates(fit_center_only)[20:22, -c(1:3)], nd = 7)
#>           label        est        se          z    pvalue   ci.lower   ci.upper
#> 20 x1.indirect. -0.0105864 0.0511619 -0.2069194 0.8360728 -0.1108619  0.0896891
#> 21 x1.indirect2 -0.2642901 0.0847350 -3.1190213 0.0018145 -0.4303676 -0.0982127
#> 22 x1.indirect3  0.2431174 0.0821712  2.9586699 0.0030897  0.0820648  0.4041699
print(parameterEstimates(fit_std)[20:22, -c(1:3)], nd = 7)
#>           label        est        se          z    pvalue   ci.lower   ci.upper
#> 20 x1.indirect. -0.0105864 0.0511619 -0.2069194 0.8360728 -0.1108619  0.0896891
#> 21 x1.indirect2 -0.2642901 0.0847350 -3.1190213 0.0018145 -0.4303676 -0.0982127
#> 22 x1.indirect3  0.2431174 0.0821712  2.9586699 0.0030897  0.0820648  0.4041699

You can see that both methods yield identical point estimates, SEs, zs, p-values, and, naturally, CIs because it is based on SEs.

There are a few issues that are important:

1) The indirect effects above are *not* the standardized indirect effects. They are unstandardized indirect effects conditional on three values of the moderator (Z2).

2) I did not add some covariances recommended by others (we should) and I did not use a label to get the variance of Z2 in the first method because I need to ensure that Z2 (as well as other "x-variables" are fixed by "fixed.x = TRUE", the default). Therefore, Z2 and the product term are treated as fixed (incorrectly, for the product term), as shown in the parameter estimates output (SE zero for them)

parameterEstimates(fit_center_only)[17:19, ]
#>      lhs op   rhs label     est se  z pvalue ci.lower ci.upper
#> 17 Z.int ~~ Z.int       452.021  0 NA     NA  452.021  452.021
#> 18 Z.int ~~    Z2         1.037  0 NA     NA    1.037    1.037
#> 19    Z2 ~~    Z2        28.904  0 NA     NA   28.904   28.904
parameterEstimates(fit_std)[17:19, ]
#>          lhs op       rhs label    est se  z pvalue ci.lower ci.upper
#> 17 Z.std.int <http://Z.std.int> ~~ Z.std.int <http://Z.std.int>       15.482  0 NA     NA   15.482   15.482
#> 18 Z.std.int <http://Z.std.int> ~~    Z2_std        0.036  0 NA     NA    0.036    0.036
#> 19    Z2_std ~~    Z2_std        0.990  0 NA     NA    0.990    0.990

This is why the indirect effects based on the two methods have identical SEs and CIs in the above example. They have no sampling variation (they are treated as fixed) and change in their scale based on Z2's SD have no impact on the derived SE of the unstandardized conditional indirect effects.

However, as discussed in previous posts, these two models, merely to illustrate when the SEs etc. are the same, are incorrect due to the omitted covariances. Z2.int (or Z.std.int <http://Z.std.int>) cannot be fixed anyway because it is a product of Z1 and Z2, Z1 being an endogenous variable predicted by x1 and x2 and so cannot be a fixed variable.

The complication is, once those omitted covariances are added, even Z2 is no long fixed variable. I will see if I have time to post another message on this case later.

3) There are cases in which we can treat even the product term as fixed: When moderation occurs on the a-path (e.g., Z2*x1). If this is the case for the model, then both Z2, x1, ans Z2*x1 can be treated as fixed (they are, by default).

Pardon me for the long examples. Several factors are involved in this issue. But I would also love to see a simpler and more straightforward way to explain this issue.

-- Shu Fai

