> > I just have a question regarding R functions -- for standardizing variables in SEM, I learned in the stat class that I can just use standardziedSolution().
> > Are there any differences between centering the variables before the analysis using scale(), and centering it after the analysis using standardizedSolution()?
> >
> > In fact, I tried both of them, and sometimes the results are really different. I am curious why it does happen.
> >
> > Best,
> > Heysung.
> >
> > 2022년 10월 7일 금요일 오전 6시 51분 46초 UTC-5에 Wen L.님이 작성:
> > > Hi Shu Fai,
> > >
> > > Thanks for catching my error. I meant to say Z2 should be _standardized_ (mean centered with unit variance). Modifying your code slightly gives the desired indirect effects:
> > > ----
> > > set.seed(87452)
> > > n <- 10000
> > >
> > > x1 <- rnorm(n)
> > > x2 <- rnorm(n)
> > > Z1 <- 1 * x1 + .4 * x2 + rnorm(n, 0, .8)
> > > Z2 <- rnorm(n)
> > > Y <- 1 * Z1 + .2 * Z2 + 1 * Z1 * Z2 + rnorm(n, 0, .9)
> > >
> > > # Arbitrarily scale them
> > > dat <- data.frame(x1 = x1 * 2,
> > >                   x2 = x2 * 3,
> > >                   Z1 = Z1 * 4,
> > >                   Z2 = Z2 * 5,
> > >                   Y = Y * 2)
> > > # Mean center all variables
> > > dat <- as.data.frame(scale(dat, center = TRUE, scale = FALSE))
> > >
> > > # standardize Z2
> > > dat$Z2 <- dat$Z2/sd(dat$Z2)
> > >
> > >
> > > # Form product term
> > > dat$Z.int <- (dat$Z1 * dat$Z2)
> > >
> > > path <-'
> > >   Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
> > >   Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2
