> > > > x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
> > > > x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
> > > > Z.int   0.000   0.000  -2.125  -0.070 452.021
> > > > Z2      0.000   0.000  -0.685   3.881   1.037  28.904
> > > >
> > > > One solution is find the SD first:
> > > >
> > > > > sd(dat$Z2)
> > > > [1]  5.403299
> > > >
> > > > We then use the SD directly in the model syntax:
> > > >
> > > > path_sd2 <-
> > > > 'Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
> > > >  Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2
> > > >  x1.indirect. := a1 * b1
> > > >  x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - 5.403299 * l)
> > > >  x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + 5.403299 * l)
> > > >  '
> > > > fit_sd2 <- sem(path_sd2, dat)
> > > > parameterEstimates(fit_sd2)
> > > >
> > > > > lavInspect(fit_sd2, "est")$psi
> > > >       Z1      Y       x1      x2      Z.int   Z2
> > > > Z1     10.540
> > > > Y       0.000   3.261
> > > > x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
> > > > x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
> > > > Z.int   0.000   0.000  -2.125  -0.070 452.021
> > > > Z2      0.000   0.000  -0.685   3.881   1.037  28.904
> > > >
> > > > Z2 is now a (fixed) X variable again (as shown in the parameter estimates table) and covaries with other (fixed) X variables.
> > > >
> > > > The argument fixed.x matters. It is TRUE by default. This leads to the next issue:
> > > >
> > > > 2) If we inspect the parameter estimates, we will notice that the covariance between the residual of Z1 and the product term Z.int is fixed to zero:
> > > >
> > > > lavInspect(fit, "est")$psi
> > > >       Z1      Y       x1      x2      Z.int   Z2
> > > > Z1     10.540
> > > > Y       0.000   3.261
> > > > x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
> > > > x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
> > > > Z.int   0.000   0.000  -2.125  -0.070 452.021
> > > > Z2      0.000   0.000  -0.685   3.881   1.037  28.904
> > > >
> > > > In the parameter estimates table, we also cannot find Z.int ~~ Z1 (or Z1 ~~ Z.int). As discussed by Kwan and Chan (2018, Table 1, Example 2), and also demonstrated by Miles, Kulesza, Ewing, Shih, Tucker, & D'Amico (2015, Model 7, Figure 6) (Miles is also a member of this group and so please correct me if I misinterpret the example), we should free this covariance:
> > > >
> > > > Kwan, J. L. Y., & Chan, W. (2018). Variable system: An alternative approach for the analysis of mediated moderation. Psychological Methods, 23(2), 262–277. https://doi.org/10.1037/met0000160
> > > >
> > > > Miles, J. N. V., Kulesza, M., Ewing, B., Shih, R. A., Tucker, J. S., & D’Amico, E. J. (2015). Moderated mediation analysis: An illustration using the association of gender with delinquency and mental health. Journal of Criminal Psychology, 5(2), 99–123. https://doi.org/10.1108/JCP-02-2015-0010
> > > >
> > > > Things get complicated here. If we add "Z.int ~~ Z1" manually, some other covariances that are automatically freed may now become fixed to zero, and this may depend on the option "fixed.x", which is TRUE by default.
> > > >
> > > > path_sd2_free <-
> > > > 'Z1 ~ a1 * x1 + a2 * x2
> > > >  Y  ~ l * Z.int + b1 * Z1 + b2 * Z2 + x1 + x2
> > > >  Z1 ~~ Z.int
> > > >  x1.indirect. := a1 * b1
> > > >  x1.indirect2 := a1 * (b1 - 5.403299 * l)
> > > >  x1.indirect3 := a1 * (b1 + 5.403299 * l)
> > > >  '
> > > > fit_sd2_free <- sem(path_sd2_free, dat)
> > > > Warning message:
> > > > In lav_partable_vnames(FLAT, "ov.x", warn = TRUE) : lavaan WARNING:
> > > >     model syntax contains variance/covariance/intercept formulas
> > > >     involving (an) exogenous variable(s): [Z.int]; These variables
> > > >     will now be treated as random introducing additional free
> > > >     parameters. If you wish to treat those variables as fixed, remove
> > > >     these formulas from the model syntax. Otherwise, consider adding
> > > >     the fixed.x = FALSE option.
> > > > parameterEstimates(fit_sd2_free)
> > > > lavInspect(fit_sd2_free, "est")$psi
> > > >
> > > > > lavInspect(fit_sd2_free, "est")$psi
> > > >       Z1      Y       x1      x2      Z.int   Z2
> > > > Z1     10.540
> > > > Y       0.000   3.261
> > > > x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
> > > > x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
> > > > Z.int  -5.682   0.000   0.000   0.000 452.021
> > > > Z2      0.000   0.000  -0.685   3.881   0.000  28.904
> > > >
> > > > Z.int now does not covary with x1, x2, and even Z2. We need to manually free some covariances. Setting fixed.x = FALSE won't free the covariances betwen Z.int and other x variables:
> > > >
> > > > > fit_sd2_free_fixedx_false <- sem(path_sd2_free, dat, fixed.x = FALSE)
> > > > > lavInspect(fit_sd2_free_fixedx_false, "est")$psi
> > > >       Z1      Y       x1      x2      Z.int   Z2
> > > > Z1     10.540
> > > > Y       0.000   3.261
> > > > x1      0.000   0.000   3.262
> > > > x2      0.000   0.000  -1.117   9.640
> > > > Z.int  -5.682   0.000   0.000   0.000 452.021
> > > > Z2      0.000   0.000  -0.685   3.881   0.000  28.904
> > > >
> > > > I found it not easy to understand cases like this in lavaan because several factors interact together. I checked the examples several times but If I misunderstood how lavaan behaves, please correct me.
> > > >
> > > > -- Shu Fai
> > > >
> > > >
> > > > On Friday, October 7, 2022 at 2:48:24 AM UTC+8 lauk...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > > > Thank you for your reply!
> > > > >
> > > > > I do have two follow-up questions.
> > > > >
> > > > > 1) So, in the total effects, should I include all indirect effects including -1 SD, mean, +1 SD? For example, regarding the total effects of X1 on Y,
> > > > >
> > > > > Z1 ~ a1*x1+a2*x2
> > > > > Y ~ I*Z.int+b1*Z1+b2*Z2
> > > > >
> > > > > #indirect effects (x1 -> Y)
> > > > > x1.indirect. := a1*b1
> > > > > x1.indirect2 := a1*(b1-1)
> > > > > x1.indirect3 := a1*(b1+1)
> > > > >
> > > > > #total effects
> > > > > x1.total := x1.indirect1+ x1.indirect2+x1.indirect3
> > > > >
> > > > > Is this correct?
> > > > >
> > > > > 2) I understand I need to include the direct effects of x1 and x2 on y, though I am not interested in them. But I wonder if those direct effects should be reported in the results section in a paper (e.g., path diagram), though I didn't hypothesize it.
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > >
> > > > > 2022년 10월 6일 목요일 오후 12시 28분 59초 UTC-5에 Wen L.님이 작성:
> > > > > > Hi,
> > > > > >
> > > > > > It depends on how your moderator Z2 is coded; specifically how to interpret a zero value. E.g., If Z2 is mean-centered, then the indirect effect of X1 on Y via Z1, at the mean value of Z2, would be a1*b1. The indirect effect of X1 on Y via Z1, at a value of Z2 one standard deviation above the mean, would be a1*(b1+I). These follow from noting that the predictors I*(Z1*Z2) + b1*Z1 = (I*Z2 + b1)*Z1 in the outcome model.
> > > > > >
> > > > > > You should also include the treatments x1 and x2 in your outcome model, e.g., 'Y ~ I*Z.int+b1*Z1+b2*Z2+c1*x1+c2*x2' even if you're not interested in the direct effects because they can be common causes of Z1 and Y. And I would suggest including confounders of Z1 and Y as well (assuming X1 and X2 are randomized) .
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Best wishes,
> > > > > > Wen Wei
> > > > > > —
> > > > > > Wen Wei Loh
> > > > > > Assistant Professor | Department of Quantitative Theory and Methods, Emory University
> > > > > > https://users.ugent.be/~weloh/
> > > > > > wen.w...@emory.edu
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > On Tuesday, October 4, 2022 at 3:45:43 PM UTC-4 lauk...@gmail.com wrote:
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Hello, I do have a really basic stat question; how to report indirect and direct effects in path analysis?
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Here is my code, with moderation.
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > data$Z.int <- (data$Z1*data$Z2)
> > > > > >
> > > > > > path <-
> > > > > > 'Z1 ~ a1*x1+a2*x2
> > > > > > Y ~ I*Z.int+b1*Z1+b2*Z2
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > #indirect effects (x1->Y)
> > > > > > x1.indirect1 := a1*b1
> > > > > > x1.indirect2 := a1*I
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > #indirect effects (x2-> Y)
> > > > > > x2.indirect1 := a2*b1
> > > > > > x2.indirect2 := a2*I
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > #total effects
> > > > > > x1.total := x1.indirect1+ x1.indirect2
> > > > > > x2.total := x2.indirect1 + x2.indirect2'
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > >
> > > > > > I wonder if this is the right way to compute indirect and total effects in path analysis with moderation.
> > > > > >
> > > > > >

